# CS509 - Assignment 1 (Single Task: GEMM + CSR Graph Conversion)

- **GEMM**: simple (direct) implementation and cache-blocked (tiled) implementation
- **CSR Graph**: adjacency-list -> Compressed Sparse Row conversion helper

## System Specs
```
Processor:Ryzen 5 4600 @ 4GHz
RAM:24GB
L1 Cache:384KB
L2 Cache:3MB
L3 Cache:8MB
```
## Project layout

```
include/gemm.h        Matrix struct, GEMM function declarations
include/csr.h          AdjacencyList / CSR structs, function declarations
src/gemm.cpp           gemm_simple, gemm_blocking, matrix I/O
src/gemm_driver.cpp    GEMM driver (reads file, times, prints per spec format)
src/csr.cpp            read_adjacency_list, build_csr, print_csr
src/csr_driver.cpp     CSR driver (reads graph file, times conversion, prints)
tools/gen_gemm_tests.py    generates gemm_test_01.txt ... at increasing sizes
tools/gen_graph_tests.py   generates graph files at V = 10/100/10000/50000/100000
tests/                 sample + generated input files
build.sh               compiles both drivers
```

## Build

```bash
./build.sh
```

## Run

```bash
# GEMM: <input file> [block_size, default 32]
./gemm_driver tests/gemm/gemm_test_01.txt 32

# CSR conversion: <graph file> 
./csr_driver tests/csr/graph_unweighted_100000.txt 
```
## Generate test files

```bash
g++ -O2 -std=c++17 -static-libgcc -static-libstdc++ -Iinclude tools/gen_gemm_tests.cpp -o tools/gen_gemm_tests
g++ -O2 -std=c++17 -static-libgcc -static-libstdc++ -Iinclude tools/gen_graph_tests.cpp -o tools/gen_graph_tests

./tools/gen_gemm_tests tests/gemm
./tools/gen_graph_tests tests/graphs
```
## Timing methodology

- The timer (`std::chrono::high_resolution_clock`) starts immediately before
  calling `gemm_simple` / `gemm_blocking` / `build_csr`, and stops immediately
  after. File reading, parsing, and result printing are excluded, per the
  assignment's timing rule.
- CSR conversion time is reported **separately** from any graph algorithm's
  time, since it is preprocessing.
- Each driver run also includes a correctness check: the GEMM driver
  verifies `gemm_simple` and `gemm_blocking` produce identical result
  matrices (warns on the console if they don't); 
